{
  "contract": "chat_receipt_projection_v7",
  "note": "DEV-030. Generated from the server's own _operation_projection; the iOS client reads this file rather than a copy. Both sides must agree case by case, because the client's only reason to say 已写入 is a record_id in one of these bodies, and an indeterminate/manual-review result must not release the durable idempotency slot. v3 adds query_result: a succeeded finance.query_expenses receipt carries the whitelisted projection plus a deterministic answer fallback, and the client renders it as a structured card rather than prose. v4 pins query_evidence_tools: the server derives it from the IR (governed read whose output contract is the expense query projection), and the client holds its hard-coded `tool == \"finance.query_expenses\"` check against it, so a rename or a second governed query fails both suites before it reaches a user. v5 adds `record` (G1): a governed write's receipt now carries the written ledger row -- 名称/原始金额/日期/是否家庭支出/分类, plus 个人支出 when the Base formula had been evaluated at read-back. It is strictly subordinate to record_id and is emitted only alongside one, so a card can never show an amount for a write the same body did not state as recorded; an idempotent_replay and an envelope that will not open both resolve to an absent record, and the client falls back to the status row rather than inventing fields. expense_categories pins the single-select option set a client may offer when editing 分类, so the picker cannot drift from the ledger's own options -- the connector never creates one. v6 adds calendar.create_event to record_evidence_tools: the device-executed calendar write is R2, so its succeeded receipt carries record_id = the device-reported event_id and renders as 已写入 through the same recorded path as a Finance write; the event carries no `record` fields, and the client must fall back to the status row when event_id is absent. operation_states is unchanged. v7 adds `domain` (design §10, gap 4): every receipt states which domain its tool belongs to -- read from the tool's IR contract, never a list beside it -- so the client can pick the 人工核对 card by domain instead of guessing from a tool name, and a null domain means no tool was recorded. v7 also pins the calendar list card (design §9.2): a succeeded calendar.query_events receipt carries `query_result` whose rows name their calendar (`calendar_title`, null when the device has no name for that identifier), give an all-day event's dates as its authority (start_date/end_date, the end being exclusive) with `date_anchor_unknown` when the device could not confirm the anchor, and flag a field the mirror dropped for length. The deterministic `answer` is the same projection in words. `calendar_query_evidence_tools` pins that read the way `query_evidence_tools` pins the ledger's (v4): the server derives it from the IR -- a governed, enabled read whose output contract is the mirror's -- and the client holds its own set against it, because the client's set is what decides which of the two projections `query_result` is even decoded as. The calendar query case's agreed outcome is now `answered_with_query`, not `answered`: step 6 (2026-09-10) built the list card that renders those same rows, so the case moves from the server's text summary to the structured card that summarises it. The client catching up, not a change of meaning. The version deliberately stays v7 -- v7 is the version this feature's contract was frozen at, no server-side shape changed in step 6, and the only new fact is a client-side pin. A client of the previous revision that reads this file fails `casesAgree` loudly on this one case, which is the intended signal that it predates the card.",
